Get android notification in terminal using a middle man:

phone ==[POST]==> server <==[GET]== computer

Because it uses non-secure HTTP putting it behind e.g.
Tailscale is a necessity here!

There is no guarantees of delivery, especially the fragile
nature of notifications, but there is some redundancy
built-in: a queue in the app that holds messages that
couldn't be received (up to 20k).

== REASONING

I want to get notifications from certain apps that are
unavailable through API/CLI. Most of the time, I only want a
glance to see if it's something that needs urgent fixing, or
if I can hold on for a bit. This scratches that itch.

== INSTALLATION

1. Install the app on the phone by grabbing an APK from here:

    https://github.com/skogsgren/notiforward/releases/tag/android-latest

2. Build the docker container on the server:

    git clone https://github.com/skogsgren/notiforward
    cd notiforward
    docker build -t notiforward-server:latest server

   And then see example docker-compose on what to set
   when integrating into (pre-existing)? stack.

3. On the client, clone the repo and symlink the script
   inside cli to bin, e.g:

   ln -s $(realpath cli/notiwatch) ~/.local/bin/notiwatch

   Then set environment variables to match server setup:

   export NOTIFORWARD_URL="http://<IP>:PORT"
   export NOTIFORWARD_TOKEN="long-secret-token"

   You can then launch it by running notiwatch

